[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-331601"] :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=4$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "db9cfafe-095c-5983-93ef-120b9a0ffabc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "ab81c4ed-be1e-5088-ab92-e9a3369ce608" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "418bf5a7-8e73-5736-8336-eac2ebb290b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "e66b7bb7-de58-5b63-b773-99eeb9f86408" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "587cefac-0d76-58d9-bda7-f54c1dcec7e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc37fe2-954d-5e08-a1ba-f082d6c3af58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-331743/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-331743/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-331743/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-331743/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-331743/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-331743/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331743" :question/id #uuid "a1b74dfd-adf9-57b0-bcdb-a5d1bb8aab44" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331743/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331743"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
